 ### Neural Rays (NeuRay)
-An approach that uses cost volumes estimated from input views via multi-view stereo algorithms, processed by a CNN to create feature maps, from which visibility and local features are extracted during volume rendering [S6]. A CNN is used to create feature maps G from the estimated cost volumes [S6]. During volume rendering, visibility and local features are extracted from these features and processed using MLPs to extract color and alpha [S6]. Visibility is computed as a cumulative density function written as a weighted sum of sigmoid functions [S6].
+- **Definition**: An approach that uses cost volumes estimated from input views via multi-view stereo algorithms, processed by a CNN to create feature maps, from which visibility and local features are extracted during volume rendering [S6].
+- **Steps**: A CNN is used to create feature maps G from the estimated cost volumes [S6]. During volume rendering, visibility and local features are extracted from these features and processed using MLPs to extract color and alpha [S6].
+- **Formula**: Visibility is computed as a cumulative density function written as a weighted sum of sigmoid functions [S6].
 
 ## Background
-Neural volume rendering originated with the introduction of Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) by Mildenhall et al. in 2020 [S2, unofficial][S4]. The method demonstrated that a simple MLP could represent complex 3D scenes and synthesize photorealistic novel views by optimizing the network parameters to minimize the difference between rendered and ground-truth images [S2, unofficial][S4]. Since then, the field has expanded to include various acceleration techniques (e.g., Instant NGP, DVGO), different representations (e.g., hash grids, Gaussian splats), and applications beyond static scenes, including surface reconstruction (Neuralangelo) and scientific visualization [S1, S4][S5, unofficial][S6].
+Neural volume rendering originated with the introduction of Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) by Mildenhall et al. in 2020 [S2, S14, unofficial]. NeRF demonstrated that a simple MLP, when trained with differentiable volume rendering, could synthesize high-quality novel views of complex scenes. The field has since expanded to include various representations such as Direct Voxel Grid Optimization (DVGO) [S1][S2, unofficial], Instant NGP [S2, unofficial], and Gaussian Splatting variants [S1][S2, unofficial]. It is widely used in computer vision for 3D reconstruction, novel view synthesis, and scientific visualization [S1][S2, S3, unofficial][S4][S5, unofficial][S6].
 
